00:08:18,050 --> 00:08:27,130
Okay, this first phrase is a very useful one if you're trying to ask someone about maybe how they're feeling or someone who's had some problems recently.

131
00:08:27,250 --> 00:08:29,570
You can ask them how things are going.

132
00:08:30,650 --> 00:08:34,730
One person says to the other, hi Daniel, how are you holding up?

133
00:08:35,250 --> 00:08:35,690
Right.

134
00:08:36,030 --> 00:08:37,390
How are you holding up?

135
00:08:37,610 --> 00:08:44,850
Now you know that to hold up something means to have it in your hands, but in this case you're talking about how are things going?

136
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:47,420
Right, but I wouldn't say, hey, how are you holding up?

137
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:48,040
Right.

138
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:49,320
You wouldn't say that to somebody, right?

139
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,480
If normal circumstances.

140
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:53,360
If Marco looks really happy, I won't ask him.

141
00:08:53,960 --> 00:09:08,000
But if someone has passed through a very difficult period, for example, if someone dies in their family or perhaps if they've been very sick or there's some negative or difficult things that happened, you can say, how are you holding up?

142
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:14,450
So basically means how are you dealing with this difficult situation or how are you dealing with your illness?

143
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:16,970
How are you holding up?

144
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:19,200
Or how are you holding up with your in-laws?

145
00:09:19,850 --> 00:09:20,370
Right, exactly.

146
00:09:20,970 --> 00:09:22,350
How are you dealing with that difficult situation?

147
00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:36,240
And then the person complemented this by saying, and this is a very good phrase, this whole sentence is very useful if you want to say something nice to a person that has recently lost someone.

148
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:39,540
You say, I am greatly sorry for your loss.

149
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:43,780
Okay, so you might have heard the word loss before, lose, loss.

150
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:45,580
Loss is a noun, it's a thing.

151
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:54,720
We normally say this about people who have died because you don't want to say, I'm really sorry for your dead brother.

152
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:56,370
It's sad, it's very rude, too direct.

153
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:04,210
So in English we say, I'm greatly sorry for your loss, for this part of your life that has gone away.

154
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:07,550
You can also say, I'm very sorry for your loss.

155
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:15,350
This is a standard phrase for funerals and memorial services.

156
00:10:16,510 --> 00:10:22,590
So if somebody's brother died, you go up to that person and you can say, I am greatly sorry for your loss.

157
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:24,960
I am very sorry for your loss.

158
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:25,410
That's what you say.

159
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:28,080
And it's very respectful and it's very nice to say.

160
00:10:28,110 --> 00:10:28,970
Yes, it's very nice.

